course: dictionary
lesson: 13442
title: Hosepipe and the garden
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A hosepipe is a long, bendy tube that carries water from a tap to wherever you need it, such as a garden or a car. The word is made of two parts: hose, a tube that carries liquid, and pipe, another tube. «Dad attached the hosepipe to the tap to water the roses.» The sentence tells you it joins to a tap and sprays water on plants.
explain: Look at the words around hosepipe to work out its meaning. «Sam turned on the hosepipe and washed the muddy bike.» Turned on and washed tell you that water is flowing through it. A hosepipe is for water, not for food or sound. Always ask: what could this word be doing in the sentence?
explain: Some words have a different name in different places. In the UK and Ireland, people say hosepipe. «In Australia, people usually just say hose.» The thing is the same, but the word changes from place to place. A dictionary tells you which places use each word.
fact: A hosepipe is a long, bendy rubber or plastic tube for carrying water.
fact: People use a hosepipe to water gardens and wash cars.
fact: «Hosepipe ban» means a rule against using hosepipes, usually in a drought, to save water.
fact: In the US, Canada and Australia, people usually just say «hose».
fact: In summer, children may play with hosepipes and sprinklers, but water is precious, so always ask before you use one.
exercise: «Dad attached the hosepipe to the tap to water the roses.» What is a hosepipe? | A cloud that brings rain | *A long, bendy tube for carrying water | A tall tree with pink flowers | A small spade for digging
exercise: «The council announced a hosepipe ban because the rain had not fallen for weeks.» What is a hosepipe ban? | A prize for the tidiest garden | *A rule against using hosepipes to save water in a drought | A way to make the rain fall faster | A party held in the summer
exercise: In the US, Canada and Australia, what do people usually say instead of hosepipe? | watering can | garden | *hose | sprinkler
test: «Sam turned on the hosepipe and washed the muddy bike.» What did Sam use the hosepipe for? | To make a snowman | *To wash his bike | To cook his dinner | To read a book
test: People in Australia often say «hose» instead of hosepipe. What does the word hosepipe mean in the UK? | A cup for drinking | A kind of bird | *A long, bendy tube for water | A small bridge over a stream
exam: «During the drought, the hosepipe ban meant we could not wash the car.» Why could the family not wash the car with a hosepipe? | *Because there was a drought and a rule saved water | Because cars do not need washing | Because hosepipes only work in winter | Because the car was too small
